hEDS Dysautonomia/Visceral Phenotype
Complex Fatigue Entities
Collagen defect affecting autonomic function

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-019
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of intractable vomiting, severe abdominal pain, and orthostatic collapse to rule out bowel obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported severe inability to keep any food or fluids down for 12 hours, accompanied by severe dizziness on standing. Immediate on-site assessment was mandated to rule out mechanical bowel obstruction or severe hypovolemic shock, and to provide parenteral antiemetics to establish safe oral rehydration before standard hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My stomach is completely paralyzed; I am throwing up everything, and the room spins violently if I try to stand.
History of Present Illness: Patient with known hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome presents with an acute visceral flare. Over the past 24 hours, the patient developed severe symptoms of gastroparesis, intense nausea, and bloating. Additionally, they report significant pelvic floor dysfunction, being unable to completely void their bladder. This is superimposed on their baseline chronic fatigue. They have been unable to retain fluids, leading to marked dysautonomia and presyncope upon standing.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively vomiting into a basin.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe dehydration and electrolyte derangement; extreme fall risk due to dysautonomia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 supine, 85/50 standing mmHg | HR: 110 supine, 135 standing bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Pale, diaphoretic, dry mucous membranes, visibly exhausted.
Physical Exam: Marked postural tachycardia and orthostatic hypotension demonstrated. Abdomen is distended, tympanic, with high-pitched, sluggish bowel sounds. No focal peritonism, rebound, or guarding. Bladder is palpable just above the symphysis pubis. Skin exhibits classic hEDS hyperextensibility. Findings align with a collagen defect affecting autonomic and visceral function.
Environmental Observations: Multiple basins of non-bilious, non-bloody vomitus near the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Unable to tolerate regular oral medications for 24 hours.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: hEDS Dysautonomia/Visceral Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Mechanical bowel obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fecal vomiting, patient passing flatus earlier in the day, and lack of localized rigid peritonitis.

Acute gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever or diarrhea, and clear history of chronic, fluctuating dysmotility.

Diabetic ketoacidosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal capillary blood glucose (tested on-site: 5.5 mmol/L) and absence of Kussmaul respirations.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's severe dysautonomia and intractable vomiting created a precarious clinical scenario with an imminent risk of hypovolemic shock. Urgent in-home evaluation was necessary to physically distinguish a functional dysmotility flare from a surgical bowel obstruction. Having confirmed functional gastroparesis, parenteral administration of antiemetics was urgently required to break the vomiting cycle and secure oral hydration. Deferring care would have guaranteed a 000 call for syncopal collapse or severe dehydration.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Prochlorperazine 12.5mg IM to bypass the paralyzed upper GI tract and halt vomiting. Targeted intervention strategy includes PEA, LDN, and Magnesium. Advised strict positional management (lie flat).
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically sipping exactly 15ml of electrolyte fluid every 15 minutes once the nausea subsides, you will facilitate continuous absorption without triggering stomach stretch receptors, directly rehydrating your body and rewarding yourself with resolved dizziness.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Shower chair strongly recommended for all bathing due to orthostatic instability.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 24-48 hours to assess hydration status and optimize prokinetic agents.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation via 000 if patient begins vomiting dark green bile or blood, if severe abdominal pain becomes sharp and localized, or if actual fainting occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute severe dysmotility requires careful monitoring; if oral intake cannot be maintained despite IM medication, IV hydration will be necessary.
Clinical Course: The gastroparetic flare should slowly ease over 24-48 hours. Dysautonomia will persist until fluid volume is fully restored.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you vomit blood, if your stomach pain suddenly becomes agonizing and sharp, or if you pass out completely.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your stomach muscles have temporarily stopped contracting due to your connective tissue disorder; the injection directly targets the brain's nausea center, allowing you to drink fluids while the stomach resets.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to eat solid food tomorrow; strictly adhering to a liquid-only diet reduces the workload on your paralyzed stomach, allowing the visceral nerves to calm down and recover faster.

